--! JavaScript itself, for the code that runs beside the compiled program:
--! a platform's runtime written in beni rather than in a sibling
--! (`docs/design/research/47-js-intrinsics-spike.md`, a spike).
--!
--! **Only a platform package or core may import this module.** Anywhere
--! else `import Js` is `js_outside_platform`: nothing here is checked, and
--! the wall that fences `foreign` fences this too (`boundary.md` §2, §4).
--!
--! **A call is compiled to the JavaScript it names and nothing else**: no
--! function is called, no value is wrapped. `get o "f"` is `o.f`,
--! `set o "f" v` is the statement `o.f = v`, `same a b` is `a === b` and
--! `call o "m" [a, b]` is `o.m(a, b)`. The declarations below are `foreign`
--! so that the checker types every use; `Js.js` holds the same operations
--! as functions, for the one use no call site covers — a declaration
--! passed as a value — and for check 2, which counts them.
--!
--! A property name is never renamed, and neither is a global.

--| Any JavaScript value, opaque to beni: `from` makes one of any beni
--| value and `to` reads one back, and neither checks anything.
pub foreign type Value


--| `null`.
pub foreign pure null : Value


--| `undefined`.
pub foreign pure undefined : Value


--| The value itself, seen as a JavaScript value. Compiled to nothing.
pub foreign pure from : a -> Value


--| The JavaScript value read back as whatever the caller says it is.
--| Compiled to nothing, and unchecked: the caller answers for the type.
pub foreign pure to : Value -> a


--| `a === b`.
pub foreign pure same : a, a -> Bool


--| `v === null`.
pub foreign pure isNull : Value -> Bool


--| `v === undefined`.
pub foreign pure isUndefined : Value -> Bool


--| `v == null`: null or undefined.
pub foreign pure isNullish : Value -> Bool


--| `a & b`.
pub foreign pure bitAnd : Int, Int -> Int


--| `globalThis.name`.
pub foreign impure global : String -> Value


--| `o.name`, or `o[name]` when the name is not a literal identifier.
pub foreign impure get : Value, String -> Value


--| `o.name = v`, a statement.
pub foreign impure set : Value, String, Value -> ()


--| `o.name(a, b)`: the arguments are written as a list literal, one
--| JavaScript argument per element.
pub foreign impure call : Value, String, List Value -> Value


--| `f(a, b)`: the arguments are written as a list literal.
pub foreign impure apply : Value, List Value -> Value


--| `new C(a, b)`: an object made by a constructor, the arguments written
--| as a list literal. `Js.construct (Js.global "Error") [ Js.from m ]` is
--| `new Error(m)`.
pub foreign impure construct : Value, List Value -> Value


--| `[a, b]`: a new JavaScript array, its elements written as a list
--| literal.
pub foreign impure array : List Value -> Value


--| `for (const x of xs) f(x)`: `f` for each element of an iterable, in
--| order. Written with a lambda, `Js.each xs (\x -> …)`, the lambda's body
--| is the loop's body.
pub foreign impure each : Value, (Value -> ()) -> ()


--| `o[k]`: an element of an array, or of anything indexed by number.
pub foreign impure at : Value, Int -> Value


--| `o[k] = v`, a statement: an element of an array written.
pub foreign impure setAt : Value, Int, Value -> ()


--| `throw v`, a statement. Nothing after it runs.
pub foreign impure throw : Value -> a


--| A mutable cell holding an `a`: `ref` makes one, `read` reads it and
--| `write` replaces what it holds. Where a cell never leaves the code that
--| made it — every use of its name is `read` or `write`, in its function or
--| in a closure made there — it is compiled to a plain `let`, a read to the
--| name and a write to an assignment; anywhere else it is `{ v }`
--| (`backend.md` §4, *A `Js.Ref` that does not escape is a `let`*).
pub foreign type Ref a


--| A new cell holding `v`.
pub foreign pure ref : a -> Ref a


--| What the cell holds now.
pub foreign impure read : Ref a -> a


--| Make the cell hold `v`, a statement.
pub foreign impure write : Ref a, a -> ()


--| `try { body() } finally { cleanup() }`: the body's value, and the
--| cleanup run after the body however it ends — when it returns, and when
--| it throws, before the throw goes on. Written with lambdas,
--| `Js.finally (\() -> …) (\() -> …)`, each lambda's body is its block and
--| no function is made. Both are `sync`: a body that suspended would leave
--| the guard before its rest ran (`backend.md` §4, *`Js.finally` is `try …
--| finally`*).
pub foreign impure finally : sync (() -> a), sync (() -> ()) -> a


--| `True` in a development build and `False` under `--release`. An `if`
--| on it keeps only the branch the build takes: the other is neither
--| written nor reached, so a declaration only it names costs the other
--| build nothing — a development crash screen ships in no release build
--| (`backend.md` §4, *`Js.development` is the build's mode*).
pub foreign pure development : Bool
